DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       YYaacccc reads the grammar specification in the file _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e and generates
       an  LALR(1)  parser  for  it.   The parsers consist of a set of LALR(1)
       parsing tables and a driver routine written in the C  programming  lan‐
       guage.  YYaacccc normally writes the parse tables and the driver routine to
       the file _y_._t_a_b_._c.

       The following options are available:

       --bb _f_i_l_e___p_r_e_f_i_x
            The --bb option changes the prefix  prepended  to  the  output  file
            names to the string denoted by _f_i_l_e___p_r_e_f_i_x_.  The default prefix is
            the character _y_.

       --BB   create a backtracking parser (compile-time configuration for  bbttyy‐‐
            aacccc).

       --dd   causes  the  header  file yy..ttaabb..hh to be written.  It contains #de‐
            fine's for the token identifiers.

       --hh   print a usage message to the standard error.

       --HH _d_e_f_i_n_e_s___f_i_l_e
            causes #define's for the token identifiers to be  written  to  the
            given _d_e_f_i_n_e_s___f_i_l_e rather than the yy..ttaabb..hh file used by the --dd op‐
            tion.

       --gg   The --gg option causes a  graphical  description  of  the  generated
            LALR(1) parser to be written to the file yy..ddoott in graphviz format,
            ready to be processed by ddoott(1).

       --ii   The --ii option causes a supplementary header  file  yy..ttaabb..ii  to  be
            written.   It  contains extern declarations and supplementary #de‐
            fine's as needed to map the conventional _y_a_c_c yyyy-prefixed names to
            whatever  the --pp option may specify.  The code file, e.g., yy..ttaabb..cc
            is modified to #include this file as well as the yy..ttaabb..hh file, en‐
            forcing consistent usage of the symbols defined in those files.

            The  supplementary header file makes it simpler to separate compi‐
            lation of lex- and yacc-files.

       --ll   If the --ll option is not specified, yyaacccc will insert  _#_l_i_n_e  direc‐
            tives  in the generated code.  The _#_l_i_n_e directives let the C com‐
            piler relate errors in the generated code to the  user's  original
            code.   If  the  --ll  option is specified, yyaacccc will not insert the
            _#_l_i_n_e directives.  _#_l_i_n_e directives specified by the user will  be
            retained.

       --LL   enable  position processing, e.g., “%locations” (compile-time con‐
            figuration for bbttyyaacccc).

       --oo _o_u_t_p_u_t___f_i_l_e
            specify the filename for the parser file.  If this option  is  not
            given,  the  output  filename is the file prefix concatenated with
            the file suffix, e.g., yy..ttaabb..cc.  This overrides the --bb option.

       --pp _s_y_m_b_o_l___p_r_e_f_i_x
            The --pp option changes the prefix prepended to yacc-generated  sym‐
            bols  to  the string denoted by _s_y_m_b_o_l___p_r_e_f_i_x_.  The default prefix
            is the string yyyy..

       --PP   create a reentrant parser, e.g., “%pure-parser”.

       --rr   The --rr option causes yyaacccc to produce separate files for  code  and
            tables.   The  code file is named _y_._c_o_d_e_._c, and the tables file is
            named _y_._t_a_b_._c.  The prefix “_y_.” can be overridden using the --bb op‐
            tion.

       --ss   suppress  “##ddeeffiinnee”  statements generated for string literals in a
            “%%ttookkeenn” statement, to more closely match original yyaacccc behavior.

            Normally when yyaacccc sees a line such as

              %token OP_ADD "ADD"

            it notices that the quoted “ADD” is a valid C identifier, and gen‐
            erates a #define not only for OP_ADD, but for ADD as well, e.g.,

              #define OP_ADD 257
              #define ADD 258

            The  original yyaacccc does not generate the second “##ddeeffiinnee”.  The --ss
            option suppresses this “##ddeeffiinnee”.

            POSIX (IEEE 1003.1 2004) documents  only  names  and  numbers  for
            “%%ttookkeenn”, though original yyaacccc and bison also accept string liter‐
            als.

       --tt   The --tt option changes the  preprocessor  directives  generated  by
            yyaacccc so that debugging statements will be incorporated in the com‐
            piled code.

            YYaacccc sends debugging output to  the  standard  output  (compatible
            with  both  the original yyaacccc and bbttyyaacccc), while bbttyyaacccc writes de‐
            bugging output to the standard error (like bbiissoonn).

       --vv   The --vv option causes a human-readable description of the generated
            parser to be written to the file _y_._o_u_t_p_u_t_.

       --VV   print the version number to the standard output.

       --yy   yyaacccc  ignores  this  option,  which  bison supports for ostensible
            POSIX compatibility.

       The _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e parameter is not optional.  However, yyaacccc accepts a single
       “-”  to read the grammar from the standard input.  A double “--” marker
       denotes the end of options.  A single _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e  parameter  is  expected
       after a “--” marker.

EEXXTTEENNSSIIOONNSS
       YYaacccc  provides  some  extensions for compatibility with bison and other
       implementations of yacc.  It accepts several _l_o_n_g  _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  which  have
       equivalents  in  yacc.   The  %%ddeessttrruuccttoorr  and  %%llooccaattiioonnss features are
       available only if yyaacccc has been configured and compiled to support  the
       back-tracking  (bbttyyaacccc)  functionality.  The remaining features are al‐
       ways available:

        %%ccooddee _k_e_y_w_o_r_d { _c_o_d_e }
              Adds the indicated source _c_o_d_e at a given point  in  the  output
              file.  The optional _k_e_y_w_o_r_d tells yyaacccc where to insert the _c_o_d_e:

              ttoopp  just  after  the  version-definition in the generated code-
                   file.

              rreeqquuiirreess
                   just after the declaration of public parser variables.   If
                   the  --dd option is given, the code is inserted at the begin‐
                   ning of the defines-file.

              pprroovviiddeess
                   just after the declaration of private parser variables.  If
                   the  --dd option is given, the code is inserted at the end of
                   the defines-file.

              If no _k_e_y_w_o_r_d is given, the code is inserted at the beginning of
              the  section of code copied verbatim from the source file.  Mul‐
              tiple %%ccooddee directives may be given; yyaacccc inserts those into the
              corresponding  code-  or defines-file in the order that they ap‐
              pear in the source file.

        %%ddeebbuugg
              This has the same effect as the “-t” command-line option.

        %%ddeessttrruuccttoorr { _c_o_d_e } _s_y_m_b_o_l_+
              defines code that is invoked when a symbol is automatically dis‐
              carded  during error recovery.  This code can be used to reclaim
              dynamically allocated memory associated with  the  corresponding
              semantic  value  for  cases where user actions cannot manage the
              memory explicitly.

              On encountering a parse error,  the  generated  parser  discards
              symbols  on  the stack and input tokens until it reaches a state
              that will allow parsing to continue.  This  error  recovery  ap‐
              proach results in a memory leak if the YYYYSSTTYYPPEE value is, or con‐
              tains, pointers to dynamically allocated memory.

              The bracketed _c_o_d_e is invoked whenever the parser  discards  one
              of  the  symbols.  Within _c_o_d_e, “$$$$” or “$$<<_t_a_g>>$$” designates the
              semantic value associated with the discarded  symbol,  and  “@@$$”
              designates its location (see %%llooccaattiioonnss directive).

              A  per-symbol  destructor is defined by listing a grammar symbol
              in _s_y_m_b_o_l_+.  A per-type destructor is defined by listing  a  se‐
              mantic  type  tag (e.g., “<some_tag>”) in _s_y_m_b_o_l_+; in this case,
              the parser will invoke _c_o_d_e whenever  it  discards  any  grammar
              symbol  that  has that semantic type tag, unless that symbol has
              its own per-symbol destructor.

              Two categories of default destructor are supported that are  in‐
              voked  when discarding any grammar symbol that has no per-symbol
              and no per-type destructor:

              •   the code for “<<**>>” is used for grammar symbols that have  an
                  explicitly declared semantic type tag (via “%%ttyyppee”);

              •   the  code  for “<<>>” is used for grammar symbols that have no
                  declared semantic type tag.

        %%eemmppttyy
              ignored by yyaacccc.

        %%eexxppeecctt _n_u_m_b_e_r
              tells yyaacccc the expected number of shift/reduce conflicts.   That
              makes it only report the number if it differs.

        %%eexxppeecctt--rrrr _n_u_m_b_e_r
              tell  yyaacccc the expected number of reduce/reduce conflicts.  That
              makes it only report the number if it differs.  This is  (unlike
              bison) allowable in LALR parsers.

        %%llooccaattiioonnss
              tells  yyaacccc to enable management of position information associ‐
              ated with each token, provided by the lexer in the global  vari‐
              able yyyylllloocc, similar to management of semantic value information
              provided in yyyyllvvaall.

              As for semantic values, locations can be referenced  within  ac‐
              tions  using  @@$$  to refer to the location of the left hand side
              symbol, and @@_N (_N an integer) to refer to the location of one of
              the  right hand side symbols.  Also as for semantic values, when
              a rule is matched, a default action is used the compute the  lo‐
              cation  represented  by  @@$$ as the beginning of the first symbol
              and the end of the last symbol in the right  hand  side  of  the
              rule.   This  default  computation can be overridden by explicit
              assignment to @@$$ in a rule action.

              The type of yyyylllloocc is YYYYLLTTYYPPEE, which is defined by default as:

              typedef struct YYLTYPE {
                  int first_line;
                  int first_column;
                  int last_line;
                  int last_column;
              } YYLTYPE;

              YYYYLLTTYYPPEE can be redefined by the user (YYYYLLTTYYPPEE__IISS__DDEEFFIINNEEDD must be
              defined,  to inhibit the default) in the declarations section of
              the specification file.  As in bison, the  macro  YYYYLLLLOOCC__DDEEFFAAUULLTT
              is  invoked  each time a rule is matched to calculate a position
              for the left hand side of the rule, before the associated action
              is executed; this macro can be redefined by the user.

              This  directive  adds  a YYYYLLTTYYPPEE parameter to yyyyeerrrroorr(()).  If the
              %%ppuurree--ppaarrsseerr directive is present, a YYYYLLTTYYPPEE parameter is  added
              to yyyylleexx(()) calls.

        %%lleexx--ppaarraamm { _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_-_d_e_c_l_a_r_a_t_i_o_n }
              By default, the lexer accepts no parameters, e.g., yyyylleexx(()).  Use
              this directive to add parameter declarations for your customized
              lexer.

        %%ppaarrssee--ppaarraamm { _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_-_d_e_c_l_a_r_a_t_i_o_n }
              By  default,  the parser accepts no parameters, e.g., yyyyppaarrssee(()).
              Use this directive to add parameter declarations for  your  cus‐
              tomized parser.

        %%ppuurree--ppaarrsseerr
              Most variables (other than yyyyddeebbuugg and yyyynneerrrrss) are allocated on
              the stack within yyyyppaarrssee, making  the  parser  reasonably  reen‐
              trant.

        %%ttookkeenn--ttaabbllee
              Make  the parser's names for tokens available in the yyyyttnnaammee ar‐
              ray.  However, yyaacccc does not predefine “$end”, “$error” or “$un‐
              defined” in this array.

PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       According to Robert Corbett,

              Berkeley Yacc is an LALR(1) parser generator.  Berkeley Yacc
              has been made as compatible as possible with AT&T Yacc.
              Berkeley Yacc can accept any input specification that
              conforms to the AT&T Yacc documentation.  Specifications
              that take advantage of undocumented features of AT&T Yacc
              will probably be rejected.

       The rationale in

              http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/yacc.html

       documents  some  features of AT&T yacc which are no longer required for
       POSIX compliance.

       That said, you may be interested in reusing  grammar  files  with  some
       other  implementation  which is not strictly compatible with AT&T yacc.
       For instance, there is bison.  Here are a few differences:

       •   YYaacccc accepts an equals mark preceding the left curly  brace  of  an
           action (as in the original grammar file ffttpp..yy):

                  |   STAT CRLF
                      = {
                              statcmd();
                      }

       •   YYaacccc  and bison emit code in different order, and in particular bi‐
           son makes forward reference to common functions such as yylex,  yy‐
           parse and yyerror without providing prototypes.

       •   Bison's  support  for “%expect” is broken in more than one release.
           For best results using bison, delete that directive.

       •   Bison has no equivalent for some of  yyaacccc's  command-line  options,
           relying on directives embedded in the grammar file.

       •   Bison's  “--yy”  option  does  not affect bison's lack of support for
           features of AT&T yacc which were deemed obsolescent.

       •   YYaacccc accepts multiple parameters with %%lleexx--ppaarraamm  and  %%ppaarrssee--ppaarraamm
           in two forms

              {type1 name1} {type2 name2} ...
              {type1 name1,  type2 name2 ...}

           Bison  accepts  the  latter (though undocumented), but depending on
           the release may generate bad code.

       •   Like bison, yyaacccc will add parameters specified via %%ppaarrssee--ppaarraamm  to
           yyyyppaarrssee,  yyyyeerrrroorr  and (if configured for back-tracking) to the de‐
           structor declared using %%ddeessttrruuccttoorr.  Bison puts the additional pa‐
           rameters  _f_i_r_s_t  for  yyyyppaarrssee and yyyyeerrrroorr but _l_a_s_t for destructors.
           YYaacccc matches this behavior.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       If there are rules that are never reduced, the number of such rules  is
       reported  on  standard  error.  If there are any LALR(1) conflicts, the
       number of conflicts is reported on standard error.



